{"context":"First planning session for walkie, held 2026-08-20 between Marij Mokoginta and an AI agent following the EKA discussion flow (idea → discuss → propose → validate → approve → publish).\n\nStarting state: the target directory was empty and not an EKA repository; `eka sync` refused with 'no eka.yaml'. The EKA workspace itself was healthy, with four unrelated projects registered. A duplicate and conflict check across the populated Discovery domains of the `eka` and `nest-desacorp` projects returned no matches for tailscale, voice, chat, messaging, call or mesh — so this was confirmed greenfield, with no existing knowledge in force to contradict.\n\nInput was a feature brief covering three core features (text messaging, voice call, presence), eight additional feature groups, technical requirements, and ten open technical questions. The session's job was to reduce that brief to decided knowledge with an honest scope.\n\nRepository bootstrapped during the session via `eka init --project walkie --namespace walkie`; identity is now frozen by the first sync.","notes":"## The finding that reshaped the brief\n\nThe brief assumed walkie would need NAT traversal, its own end-to-end encryption, and a relay server. Investigation of the tailnet substrate established that three of those are already provided — traversal with relay fallback, transport encryption, and authenticated peer identity retrievable server-side — which removed an authentication subsystem and a traversal implementation from the scope entirely, and narrowed encryption to the coordinator's two blind spots. This is recorded as fnd:tailnet-capability-baseline and is the evidence behind adr:001 and adr:004.\n\n## Decisions taken, with the alternatives that were live at the time\n\n**Identity.** Options were a shared `tools` project with a per-repo namespace, a standalone project, or initialising in the parent directory. Chosen: standalone — project, name and namespace all `walkie`. Rationale given: the tool is expected to stand on its own rather than be internal tooling under a shared umbrella.\n\n**Transport topology.** The agent recommended coordinator-only with a pure peer-to-peer data plane, on the grounds of least code, lowest latency and the smallest encryption scope, and noted that a relay fallback partially duplicates the tailnet's own DERP fallback. **The decision-maker chose hybrid** — direct with relay fallback — accepting the doubled test surface in exchange for a defined recourse when a direct path cannot form, including the case of a degraded local Tailscale daemon. The disagreement is recorded because it is the reason adr:001 carries an unusually explicit consequences section, and the reason the direct-versus-relay ratio is a required metric rather than an optional one.\n\n**Runtime stack.** Go with CGO confined to audio behind a build tag, over Rust and over a Python prototype. Rust was acknowledged as the better technical fit for the audio path; it was declined on delivery grounds for an internal tool at this scale, and adr:002 records the escape hatch (split the audio engine into its own process) rather than leaving it to be rediscovered.\n\n**MVP boundary.** Three options: text and presence only with voice deferred; text and real-time voice together as originally requested; or text plus asynchronous push-to-talk voice notes. Chosen: the third. This keeps 'voice' present in the first release while leaving the jitter buffer, loss concealment and latency target out of it.\n\n**Remote command execution.** Declined and delegated to Tailscale SSH. The agent argued that a second, less-reviewed remote-execution path is a net security regression rather than an added feature, even in its most constrained form. Recorded as adr:005 so the absence reads as a decision.\n\n**Application-encryption scope.** Limited to queued payloads at rest and relay-path payloads, rather than full end-to-end encryption of all messages and local history. The wider option was declined for the MVP because it drags in key rotation, multi-device identity and history search for a threat model the team has not stated.\n\n**Latency requirement.** Reformulated from a blanket 'under 100 ms' to 'under 100 ms on direct paths, best-effort on relayed paths, with the active path and measured latency displayed to the user'. The original figure was not testable across both paths and would have been quietly unenforceable.\n\n## Corrections the session made to the brief\n\n1. **Hold-to-talk cannot be built portably.** A terminal does not report key-release events. The portable interaction is a toggle; hold-to-talk is an opt-in enhancement only where the Kitty keyboard protocol is detected. This is a constraint, not a preference.\n2. **The MVP already needs CGO.** Voice notes require capture and Opus encoding, and no viable pure-Go encoder exists. Resolved by a build tag rather than by changing the MVP, so the default build stays pure Go and static.\n3. **Relay fallback has nothing to fall back from in the MVP.** All MVP traffic passes through the coordinator anyway, so the fallback is decided now but implemented in phase 2.\n4. **The sub-100 ms budget is calculated, not measured.** Every term in it is nominal, and no ARM device in the fleet has been profiled. A spike must produce real numbers before the requirement is treated as met.\n\n## Answers to the ten questions in the brief\n\nQuestions 2 (NAT traversal) and 3 (end-to-end encryption) were largely dissolved by fnd:tailnet-capability-baseline rather than answered. Question 7 (remote execution security) was answered by declining the feature. The remaining seven are answered inside the artifacts: voice approach and bandwidth in fnd:terminal-audio-constraints and adr:003; offline delivery in req:offline-delivery; file transfer resume in req:file-transfer and adr:003; reconnection in req:device-presence and the MVP scope; screen sharing and plugin extensibility in adr:005 and the roadmap's phases 3 and 4.\n\n## What was deliberately not decided\n\nGroup voice topology beyond 'not a full mesh'. Key rotation and multi-device identity. Rich text, emoji and attachment preview. Auto-update signing details. Phases 2 to 4 were sequenced but not decomposed, on the decision-maker's instruction, so that decomposition follows team feedback rather than preceding it.","verification":"Preflight verified with `eka status` (workspace healthy, schema v4) and `eka sync` (refusal confirming greenfield). Duplicate check run with `eka get discovery --no-content --compact` against both populated projects; the query itself was verified to return data (26 Discovery units in `eka`) so that the zero-match result was a genuine negative rather than a silent failure.\n\nRepository bootstrapped with `eka init --dry-run` inspected before the real run; the real run reported validation PASS with 0 errors and 0 warnings, and `eka sync` registered the project and wrote a snapshot.\n\nAll drafts scaffolded through `eka new --file` in batches. Author identity and change-log dates were taken by the CLI from git configuration — `Marij Mokoginta`, 2026-08-20 — and were not hand-written. Structural pre-flight checked with `eka draft list`: none of the walkie drafts carries an invalid marker.\n\nEvery artifact was scaffolded in its initial state (`draft`, or `proposed` for decision records). No state was pre-advanced during authoring; state changes are the subject of the approval gate, not of drafting."}